  "statement": "The environmental cost of AI training is a critical factor that must be considered when evaluating the overall benefits of AI development. As I have previously argued, the development of AI must be governed by a framework that prioritizes public interest, safety, and transparency. The potential economic benefits of AI development, estimated to be as high as $15.7 trillion by 2030, must be weighed against the substantial environmental costs of AI training. For instance, training a single large-scale AI model can consume as much electricity as a small town over the course of a year, resulting in significant greenhouse gas emissions and contributing to climate change.\n\nThe argument that regulators should prioritize frameworks that encourage innovation overlooks the importance of balancing economic growth with environmental sustainability. While AI development has the potential to drive economic growth and improve living standards, it is crucial that we prioritize public interest and safety in the development and deployment of AI systems. This includes investing in research and development for AI safety and risk mitigation, as well as implementing transparency and accountability mechanisms to ensure that AI systems are designed and used in ways that benefit society as a whole.\n\nFurthermore, the environmental costs of AI training are not limited to energy consumption. The production of AI hardware, such as graphics processing units (GPUs) and tensor processing units (TPUs), requires significant amounts of energy and resources, resulting in environmental degradation and pollution. Therefore, it is essential that we adopt a holistic approach to AI development, one that takes into account the environmental, social, and economic impacts of AI systems. By prioritizing public interest and safety, we can ensure that AI development is aligned with human values and promotes a more sustainable and equitable future.\n\nIn conclusion, the environmental cost of AI training is a critical consideration that must be addressed in the development and deployment of AI systems. By prioritizing public interest and safety, and investing in research and development for AI safety and risk mitigation, we can ensure that AI development is aligned with human values and promotes a more sustainable and equitable future. I note that no one has responded to my argument that the development of AI must be governed by a framework that prioritizes public interest, safety, and transparency, and I urge my opponents to engage with this point.",
